WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.310
Some agent actions should require human review before execution.

2
00:00:04.310 --> 00:00:18.957
Relevant examples include financial transactions, access changes, destructive operations, external communications, sensitive data sharing, code deployment, legal commitments, or high-impact system changes.

3
00:00:18.957 --> 00:00:26.521
The required control should reflect impact, reversibility, uncertainty, and the sensitivity of the resource involved.

4
00:00:26.521 --> 00:00:30.612
An approval gate should be meaningful rather than a vague confirmation button.

5
00:00:30.612 --> 00:00:39.801
The reviewer needs to see the exact proposed action, the target, the important data involved, the reason for the recommendation, and the expected impact.

6
00:00:39.801 --> 00:00:50.337
Approval should occur before execution, and the system should bind that approval to the reviewed action so the agent cannot silently substitute different parameters afterward.

7
00:00:50.337 --> 00:00:52.443
Low-risk work can remain efficient.

8
00:00:52.443 --> 00:01:03.927
An agent may search public documentation, summarize information, or prepare a draft without the same review required to send, delete, purchase, merge, or change access.

9
00:01:03.927 --> 00:01:12.139
Risk tiers help teams place human attention where consequences are highest instead of requiring manual approval for every harmless step.

10
00:01:12.139 --> 00:01:17.696
When required approval is missing, expired, or denied, the workflow should stop safely.

11
00:01:17.696 --> 00:01:22.605
It should not retry through a different tool or downgrade the action without policy.

12
00:01:22.605 --> 00:01:27.894
High-impact environments may also require separation of duties or a second approver.

13
00:01:27.894 --> 00:01:33.361
These controls preserve accountability while still allowing agents to automate well-bounded work.
